    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate prioritizing stolen base upside and favorable matchup splits. **Brice Turang** headlines our lineup with a 70.49 efficiency score; his matchup against Adrian Houser is primed for success, bolstered by an 18% boost from Houser\u2019s mediocre xERA and a tactical advantage created by the catcher\u2019s slower pop-time. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (69.53) remains a core play despite a 36% rain risk in his contest, as the platoon advantage against Andrew Morris is simply too productive to fade. Meanwhile, **Corbin Carroll** (67.04) continues to be our primary engine for speed; even against a tough arm in Justin Wrobleski, his 85th-percentile stolen base probability against a slow-delivering starter keeps him firmly in the \"Strong\" start tier.\n\nOur most difficult decision revolved around **Jose Caballero**, whose 58.29 score hovered just below the threshold to potentially move to the bench, yet his high-leverage speed profile against Slade Cecconi kept him in the outfield. He narrowly outperformed **Alex Freeland** (49.25), who remains our most marginal start due to the unfavorable #9 spot in the batting order. While players like **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Max Muncy**, and **Carson Williams** are looking for opportunities to break into this rotation, the current efficiency scores suggest stability is the priority. We are keeping a close watch on **Vinnie Pasquantino**\u2019s rain delay status, as an early washout could necessitate a pivot to our bench depth. We remain cautious with **Gabriel Moreno** and **Dalton Rushing**; their sub-55 scores reflect the volatility of the catcher position today, but in a 5x5 Roto format, their counting stats remain essential placeholders against their respective right-handed matchups.",
